        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where You Can Actually Put One
          </h2>

          <p>
            Placement decides how well these units perform more than the brand does, because the temperature of the surrounding air is the fuel supply. Warmer air means more efficiency and faster recovery; colder air means the resistance elements come on and your savings evaporate.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Excellent locations:</strong> an unfinished basement that stays above 60 degrees year round, a garage in a warm climate, a mechanical room that shares space with a furnace or dryer, or anywhere that is already uncomfortably warm and humid — the unit will improve the space while heating the water.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Poor locations:</strong> a sealed closet with a solid door, an unheated garage in a cold climate, a crawlspace, or anywhere directly adjacent to a bedroom wall. The first three starve it of heat; the last one starves you of sleep, because a compressor and fan running at 50 dB at two in the morning is audible through a stud wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Height and clearance:</strong> heat pump water heaters are typically six to eight inches taller than the tank they replace because of the compressor on top, and they need clearance above it for airflow and service. Measure the ceiling height, the joists, and any ductwork before ordering — this is the most common reason a delivery goes back on the truck.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Condensate:</strong> the unit produces a steady trickle of water it pulls from the air, and that has to go somewhere. A floor drain is ideal, a condensate pump is the common fallback, and no plan at all is how a basement floor gets wet. Budget for the pump if there is no drain nearby.
          </p>
        </div>

