        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running Cost and Honest Payback
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against an electric resistance tank, the payback is fast and uncontroversial: roughly $30 to $40 a month saved for a typical family, which recovers a $2,500 installed cost in five to seven years without any rebate, and often three to four years with one. Nothing else in home efficiency is that reliable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against natural gas the picture is honest rather than flattering. At average gas and electricity prices the running costs are broadly similar, and the case for switching rests on other things: eliminating a combustion appliance and its flue, removing a carbon monoxide source, cooling and drying a basement, and qualifying for electrification rebates. If your gas is cheap and your electricity is expensive, a heat pump water heater may not save you a dollar, and you should know that before you buy rather than after.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two factors swing the result more than brand choice. The first is the temperature of the space the unit lives in: the same appliance in a 70-degree mechanical room and a 45-degree garage will show a running cost difference of 30 percent or more, because the cold installation keeps falling back to resistance heat. The second is mode selection — leaving a unit in the default hybrid mode rather than the heat-pump-only or energy-saver mode gives away a meaningful share of the savings for recovery speed most households never need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="noise-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Noise, Condensate, and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            These units make noise that resistance tanks do not. Expect 49 to 60 dB at three feet, which is roughly a modern refrigerator to a window air conditioner, and it runs for hours after a heavy hot water draw rather than in short bursts. In an unfinished basement nobody cares. Directly below a bedroom, or in a condo utility closet sharing a wall with a neighbour, it can become the thing you regret. Check the decibel rating and think carefully about the wall on the other side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is genuinely light but not zero. Clean or rinse the air filter every few months — it is on the front of the unit and takes a minute, and a blocked filter directly reduces efficiency. Keep the condensate drain and trap clear. And most importantly, check the sacrificial anode rod every three to five years and replace it when it is depleted. That single task is the difference between a tank that lasts ten years and one that lasts eighteen, and it is the most neglected maintenance item in the entire house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once a year, flush a few gallons from the drain valve to clear sediment, and if your water is hard, do it more often. Beyond that, these are appliances with one moving assembly, and they are not demanding.
          </p>
        </div>


