                The Rheem ProTerra 50 gallon is our overall pick, combining a 4.07 UEF, a ten-year warranty, and the best controls in the category. For larger households the A. O. Smith Voltex AL 66 gallon solves the recovery complaint. If your electrical panel cannot take a new 240V circuit, the GE Profile UltraFast 120V plug-in is usually the cheapest workable installation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a heat pump water heater cost installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically $2,500 to $4,500 installed for a 240V hybrid, of which $1,200 to $2,600 is equipment. A 120V plug-in installs for $1,800 to $3,000 because it needs no new circuit. A CO2 split system like the Sanden runs $5,000 to $7,000. Federal, state, and utility rebates frequently stack and have covered more than half the installed cost in some states.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do heat pump water heaters work in a cold garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They work, but poorly, and the efficiency you actually get will be far below the rating. Below about 45 degrees F most integrated units fall back on their resistance elements, at which point you are running an expensive electric tank. In a genuinely cold space, either keep the unit in a heated basement or choose a CO2 split system with an outdoor compressor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a heat pump water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ten to fifteen years is the realistic range, similar to a conventional tank, and the tank rather than the heat pump is nearly always what fails. Replacing the sacrificial anode rod every three to five years is the single highest-value thing an owner can do to extend that, and it is cheap enough that skipping it makes no sense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are heat pump water heaters noisy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They produce 49 to 60 dB at three feet — comparable to a refrigerator or a window air conditioner — and they run for extended periods after heavy hot water use rather than in short bursts. In an unfinished basement this is a non-issue. Below a bedroom floor or in a condo closet against a shared wall, check the decibel rating carefully before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a heat pump water heater cool my basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and that is either a benefit or a cost depending on your climate. It removes heat and moisture from the surrounding air, which is welcome in a humid Southern basement or garage and unwelcome in a Northern one in winter, where your furnace simply replaces that heat. Factor it in honestly when estimating savings in a cold climate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a heat pump water heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The plumbing connections are within reach of a competent DIYer, but most jurisdictions require a permit and inspection, and the electrical work should be done by a licensed electrician. Self-installation also typically voids the manufacturer's warranty on a ten-year appliance. Given that the rebate paperwork usually requires a licensed installer's invoice, hiring the work out is generally the better economics.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
