        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Heat Pump Water Heater Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every bad experience with this category traces back to a decision made before the unit was delivered.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Matching the Old Tank Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Replacing a 50 gallon gas heater with a 50 gallon heat pump unit gives a worse hot water experience, because heat pumps recover more slowly. Size up one step, or read the first-hour rating and match that instead of the gallons. This single error accounts for most of the unhappy owners in this category.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting It in a Sealed Closet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These units need roughly 700 cubic feet of air to pull heat from. Installed in a tight closet with a solid door, the unit chills its own supply air, loses efficiency, and leans on the resistance elements. Fit louvered doors or a duct kit — it is a small cost that protects the entire value proposition.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Planning for Condensate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The unit will produce a steady drip of water pulled out of the air, every day, forever. Without a floor drain nearby you need a condensate pump, and discovering that after the heater is installed means a wet floor and a second service call.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Before Checking Rebates</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Utility and state rebates are usually tied to specific efficiency thresholds or an approved model list, and they can be worth over a thousand dollars. Choosing a model first and looking for money afterwards is how people end up with an ineligible unit that cost more than the eligible one would have.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Anode Rod for a Decade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The sacrificial anode is what stops the tank corroding, and it is consumed over time by design. Checking it every three to five years and replacing it for under a hundred dollars is the difference between a ten-year appliance and an eighteen-year one — on equipment that costs several thousand dollars installed.
              </p>
